          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which histological feature distinguishes ischaemic acute tubular necrosis from n</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which histological feature distinguishes ischaemic acute tubular necrosis from nephrotoxic acute tubular necrosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Diffuse necrosis throughout the proximal convoluted tubule, sparing the basement membrane</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This diffuse, basement-membrane-sparing pattern is characteristic of nephrotoxic ATN, not ischaemic ATN.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Patchy necrosis of the straight proximal tubule and medullary thick ascending limb, with tubulorrhexis (basement membrane rupture)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Ischaemic ATN patchily targets the straight PCT segment and the medullary thick ascending limb, and tubulorrhexis — rupture of the tubular basement membrane — is a defining feature not seen in nephrotoxic ATN.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dense neutrophilic infiltrate filling the interstitium</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ATN of either cause shows no significant inflammatory infiltrate — a dense neutrophilic infiltrate is a feature of pyelonephritis, not ATN.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Eosinophils and lymphocytes infiltrating the interstitium with granuloma formation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This eosinophil/lymphocyte pattern is seen in drug-induced allergic tubulointerstitial nephritis, a type IV hypersensitivity reaction, not in ATN.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review: ischaemic ATN is patchy with tubulorrhexis at the straight PCT and medullary thick ascending limb; nephrotoxic ATN is diffuse throughout the PCT and spares the basement membrane."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done — you've distinguished the patchy, tubulorrhexis-bearing ischaemic pattern from the diffuse, basement-membrane-sparing nephrotoxic pattern."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
