          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A woman with fever and flank pain has pyuria on urinalysis. Which urinary findin</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A woman with fever and flank pain has pyuria on urinalysis. Which urinary finding confirms that the infection has reached the renal parenchyma, rather than remaining confined to the bladder?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>White blood cell casts</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. WBC casts can only form within renal tubules, so their presence proves the kidney parenchyma — not just the bladder — is infected.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Numerous squamous epithelial cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Squamous cells reflect urethral or vaginal contamination of the sample, not parenchymal infection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Red blood cell casts</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>RBC casts point to glomerular bleeding, as in glomerulonephritis, not pyelonephritis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Sterile pyuria on culture</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sterile pyuria suggests non-bacterial causes such as tuberculosis — acute pyelonephritis is typically culture-positive, usually for E. coli.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: white blood cell casts form only inside renal tubules, so they are the specific marker that infection has reached the kidney parenchyma, unlike simple cystitis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right — white cell casts are the urinary proof that infection involves the renal tubules and interstitium, not just the bladder."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
