          "label": "<p>A 4-year-old develops bloody diarrhoea followed by acute kidney injury, anaemia </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 4-year-old develops bloody diarrhoea followed by acute kidney injury, anaemia with schistocytes on blood film, and thrombocytopenia. Which mechanism is most likely responsible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>An autoantibody against ADAMTS13 allowing ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers to accumulate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the mechanism of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), a systemic disorder that typically also features fever and neurological signs — not the diarrhoea-associated syndrome described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Shiga toxin from E. coli O157:H7 binding Gb3 receptors on glomerular endothelial cells</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. This is haemolytic uraemic syndrome. Children express abundant Gb3 receptors in the kidney, which is why Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli causes a renal-dominant, diarrhoea-associated illness predominantly in this age group.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deficiency of complement regulatory proteins causing unchecked alternative pathway activation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes atypical HUS, a different, typically non-diarrhoeal variant not covered by this lecture's classic Shiga-toxin HUS description.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Disseminated malignancy causing tumour-associated microangiopathy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Malignancy-associated microangiopathy is a recognised but separate cause of thrombotic microangiopathy, unrelated to the diarrhoeal prodrome described here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review: bloody diarrhoea plus the triad in a child points to Shiga-toxin-producing E. coli O157:H7 binding Gb3 receptors — this is haemolytic uraemic syndrome, distinct from the ADAMTS13-autoantibody mechanism of TTP."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly — Shiga toxin binding Gb3 on glomerular endothelium is the hallmark of classic childhood HUS."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
