          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 60-year-old smoker presents with an incidentally found left upper-pole renal m</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 60-year-old smoker presents with an incidentally found left upper-pole renal mass, unexplained polycythaemia, and hypercalcaemia. Which best explains this combination?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ectopic erythropoietin and parathyroid-hormone-related protein secretion by a renal cell carcinoma</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Renal cell carcinoma is classically paraneoplastic: ectopic erythropoietin causes polycythaemia and ectopic PTH-related protein causes hypercalcaemia, even without bone metastases.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Metastatic spread of the tumour to bone, directly causing both findings</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bony metastases could cause hypercalcaemia but would not explain polycythaemia, and the vignette describes paraneoplastic effects, not confirmed metastatic disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wilms tumour producing renin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Wilms tumour is a childhood tumour; this presentation in a 60-year-old smoker fits renal cell carcinoma, not Wilms tumour.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Angiomyolipoma causing a mass effect on the adrenal gland</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Angiomyolipoma is a benign, fat-containing tumour; it does not cause paraneoplastic polycythaemia or hypercalcaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall: renal cell carcinoma can secrete ectopic erythropoietin (polycythaemia) and PTH-related protein (hypercalcaemia) even without metastasis — this paraneoplastic pattern, with an incidental renal mass in an older smoker, is highly characteristic."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Right — ectopic EPO and PTHrP secretion are classic paraneoplastic manifestations of renal cell carcinoma, earning it the nickname 'the internist's tumour'."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
