          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>On microscopy, a small, finely granular kidney shows efferent arteriolar hyalino</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>On microscopy, a small, finely granular kidney shows efferent arteriolar hyalinosis. Which diagnosis does this single finding point to?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Benign nephrosclerosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Benign nephrosclerosis causes hyaline arteriolosclerosis of the afferent arteriole, not the efferent arteriole.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Diabetic nephropathy</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Hyalinosis of the efferent arteriole is pathognomonic for diabetic nephropathy — afferent arteriolar hyalinosis occurs in several conditions, but efferent involvement is specific to diabetes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic glomerulonephritis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
